        </section>

        <section id="misconfig-federation" class="card">
            <h2>Configuration Mapping &amp; Identity Federation</h2>
            <p>Improper cloud configurations remain a leading cause of modern system breaches. These errors occur when default access controls or overly broad firewall rules are left active on production assets, bypassing internal security perimeters.</p>
            <p>Mitigating these risks requires implementing centralized single sign-on (SSO) systems backed by Identity Federation protocols like SAML or OpenID Connect, allowing safe identity verification across distributed web services without sharing user passwords.</p>
            <div class="live-graphic-container">
                <canvas id="canvas-federation" class="live-canvas-element" width="778" height="280"></canvas>
                <div class="img-caption">Figure 16: Active Audit Scans Map Tracking Configuration Ingestions</div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- CASE STUDY -->
        <section id="case-study-sec" class="case-study">
            <h3>Cloud Breach Analysis: Case Study Breakdown</h3>
            <p>A prominent financial institution's cloud data breach serves as a stark warning regarding misconfiguration risks. A web application firewall (WAF) running with overly broad access controls was targeted by a Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) attack.</p>
            <p>By querying the internal Instance Metadata Service endpoint at the standard link-local address (<code>http://169.254.169.254</code>), the threat actor extracted high-privilege administrative IAM credentials, using them to exfiltrate over 100 million customer records directly from backend storage buckets.</p>
            <p><strong>Remediation Takeaways:</strong> Always implement strict Least-Privilege access controls, mandate the use of hardened metadata endpoints (IMDSv2) that require session tokens, and deploy continuous configuration compliance monitoring to catch anomalies immediately.</p>
